{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01XiXae3JX4QDAgHt6qw3gwX","type":"tool_result","content":"1\tid: srd001-testutils\n2\ttitle: \"pkg/testutils: Differential Testing Harness\"\n3\t\n4\tproblem: |\n5\t  Every utility in go-unix-utils is verified by running the Go binary and the Homebrew\n6\t  GNU reference binary side by side and comparing their stdout, stderr, and exit code.\n7\t  Without a shared harness, each cmd/ package would duplicate the execution and comparison\n8\t  logic, the normalization hooks needed for non-deterministic output (timestamps in ts,\n9\t  path ordering in find), and the divergence reporting format. This duplication makes\n10\t  the verification layer fragile: a bug in one cmd/'s harness copy would produce\n11\t  different verification behavior than another cmd/'s copy.\n12\t\n13\t  pkg/testutils is the acceptance gate for the entire pipeline. The cobbler orchestrator\n14\t  cannot declare a utility complete until its differential tests pass through this harness.\n15\t  This SRD is also the authoritative API reference for stitch agents implementing cmd/\n16\t  packages: the agent reads this document, knows the full pkg/testutils API, and imports\n17\t  the package without reinventing it.\n18\t\n19\t  A cmd/ test file imports pkg/testutils and defines test cases as data. The stitch agent\n20\t  producing a new cmd/ utility writes only the test file — it does not recreate the package.\n21\t  The canonical usage pattern is:\n22\t\n23\t    package main_test\n24\t\n25\t    import (\n26\t        \"os\"\n27\t        \"testing\"\n28\t        \"github.com/petar-djukic/go-unix-utils/pkg/testutils\"\n29\t    )\n30\t\n31\t    func TestDiff(t *testing.T) {\n32\t        goBin := os.Getenv(\"GO_BINARY\")\n33\t        refBin := os.Getenv(\"REF_BINARY\")\n34\t        tests := []testutils.DiffTest{\n35\t            {Name: \"basic\", Args: []string{\"-n\", \"testdata/input.txt\"}, ExitCode: 0},\n36\t            {Name: \"stdin\", Args: []string{\"-c\"}, Stdin: []byte(\"hello\\n\"), ExitCode: 0},\n37\t            {Name: \"normalized\", Args: []string{\"-s\", \"%H:%M:%S\"}, Stdin: []byte(\"event\\n\"),\n38\t                ExitCode: 0, Normalize: []testutils.NormalizeFunc{testutils.TimestampNormalizer}},\n39\t        }\n40\t        testutils.RunDiffTests(t, goBin, refBin, tests)\n41\t    }\n42\t\n43\tgoals:\n44\t  - G1: Provide a single execution function that runs a Go binary and a GNU reference binary with identical inputs and returns both outputs for comparison.\n45\t  - G2: Provide a comparison function that diffs stdout, stderr, and exit code and reports divergence with the triggering input.\n46\t  - G3: Provide a normalization hook mechanism so individual utilities can strip or replace non-deterministic output fields before comparison.\n47\t  - G4: Integrate with go test so that each cmd/ package can define test cases as data and call the harness from a standard TestXxx function.\n48\t\n49\trequirements:\n50\t  R1:\n51\t    title: Test case data structure\n52\t    items:\n53\t      - R1.1:\n54\t          text: |\n55\t            Must define DiffTest as the following named struct in package testutils:\n56\t\n57\t              type DiffTest struct {\n58\t                  Name          string                 // subtest name used with t.Run; required\n59\t                  Args          []string               // command-line arguments passed to both binaries\n60\t                  Stdin         []byte                 // nil = no stdin (both binaries receive EOF immediately)\n61\t                  Env           []string               // nil = use defaults only (LC_ALL=C); non-nil = KEY=VALUE pairs merged into inherited environment\n62\t                  WorkDir       string                 // empty = per-test t.TempDir(); non-empty = use this directory for both binaries\n63\t                  ExitCode      int                    // expected exit code for both binaries\n64\t                  Normalize     []func([]byte) []byte  // applied in order to stdout and stderr of both binaries before comparison; nil or empty = no normalization\n65\t                  ExpectedFiles map[string][]byte      // optional: path -> expected byte content after execution, for file-output utilities (sponge, cp)\n66\t              }\n67\t      - R1.2:\n68\t          text: DiffTest.Stdin nil means both binaries receive EOF immediately on stdin. An empty non-nil slice ([]byte{}) also produces no bytes but is semantically distinct (open stdin that is immediately closed).\n69\t      - R1.3:\n70\t          text: |\n71\t            DiffTest.Env nil means both binaries inherit the test process environment with\n72\t            default overrides applied (see R2.6). When non-nil, the KEY=VALUE pairs in the\n73\t            slice are merged into the inherited environment: matching keys are overridden, new\n74\t            keys are added. This merge behavior matches the struct comment (\"override or\n75\t            extend\") and avoids requiring callers to replicate the full environment for a\n76\t            single override.\n77\t      - R1.4:\n78\t          text: Must define type NormalizeFunc = func([]byte) []byte as a named type alias in package testutils. The Normalize field in DiffTest uses this element type. When the slice is nil or empty, no normalization is applied to stdout or stderr.\n79\t\n80\t  R2:\n81\t    title: Binary execution\n82\t    items:\n83\t      - R2.1:\n84\t          text: |\n85\t            Must provide func RunDiffTests(t *testing.T, goBinary, refBinary string, tests []DiffTest) that accepts the path to the Go binary under test (goBinary) and the GNU reference binary (refBinary), then runs each DiffTest as a named subtest via t.Run(test.Name, ...). Both binaries are invoked with identical Args, Stdin, and Env for each test case.\n86\t      - R2.2:\n87\t          text: Must capture stdout, stderr, and exit code from each binary independently.\n88\t      - R2.3:\n89\t          text: Must impose a configurable timeout on each binary invocation. The default timeout must be 10 seconds. The test fails if either binary exceeds the timeout.\n90\t      - R2.4:\n91\t          text: Must not write any output to the test process stdout or stderr during a passing test run.\n92\t      - R2.5:\n93\t          text: |\n94\t            Must support passing a working directory to each binary invocation via a WorkDir\n95\t            field on DiffTest. When WorkDir is empty, both binaries run in a per-test temporary\n96\t            directory created by the harness via t.TempDir(). The harness must use the same\n97\t            working directory for both binaries in a given test case. The DiffTest struct gains:\n98\t\n99\t              WorkDir string // empty = per-test t.TempDir(); non-empty = use this directory for both binaries\n100\t      - R2.6:"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"0e2149c5-f6e0-4cca-85fb-431cafcf1c4e","uuid":"782db7b1-e0df-470b-a357-5266ef0a5941","timestamp":"2026-05-13T21:45:40.375Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/private/var/folders/dl/cbns6zkx7txc7pt64b6cgzp40000gn/T/go-unix-utils-worktrees/5067/docs/specs/software-requirements/srd001-testutils.yaml","content":"id: srd001-testutils\ntitle: \"pkg/testutils: Differential Testing Harness\"\n\nproblem: |\n  Every utility in go-unix-utils is verified by running the Go binary and the Homebrew\n  GNU reference binary side by side and comparing their stdout, stderr, and exit code.\n  Without a shared harness, each cmd/ package would duplicate the execution and comparison\n  logic, the normalization hooks needed for non-deterministic output (timestamps in ts,\n  path ordering in find), and the divergence reporting format. This duplication makes\n  the verification layer fragile: a bug in one cmd/'s harness copy would produce\n  different verification behavior than another cmd/'s copy.\n\n  pkg/testutils is the acceptance gate for the entire pipeline. The cobbler orchestrator\n  cannot declare a utility complete until its differential tests pass through this harness.\n  This SRD is also the authoritative API reference for stitch agents implementing cmd/\n  packages: the agent reads this document, knows the full pkg/testutils API, and imports\n  the package without reinventing it.\n\n  A cmd/ test file imports pkg/testutils and defines test cases as data. The stitch agent\n  producing a new cmd/ utility writes only the test file — it does not recreate the package.\n  The canonical usage pattern is:\n\n    package main_test\n\n    import (\n        \"os\"\n        \"testing\"\n        \"github.com/petar-djukic/go-unix-utils/pkg/testutils\"\n    )\n\n    func TestDiff(t *testing.T) {\n        goBin := os.Getenv(\"GO_BINARY\")\n        refBin := os.Getenv(\"REF_BINARY\")\n        tests := []testutils.DiffTest{\n            {Name: \"basic\", Args: []string{\"-n\", \"testdata/input.txt\"}, ExitCode: 0},\n            {Name: \"stdin\", Args: []string{\"-c\"}, Stdin: []byte(\"hello\\n\"), ExitCode: 0},\n            {Name: \"normalized\", Args: []string{\"-s\", \"%H:%M:%S\"}, Stdin: []byte(\"event\\n\"),\n                ExitCode: 0, Normalize: []testutils.NormalizeFunc{testutils.TimestampNormalizer}},\n        }\n        testutils.RunDiffTests(t, goBin, refBin, tests)\n    }\n\ngoals:\n  - G1: Provide a single execution function that runs a Go binary and a GNU reference binary with identical inputs and returns both outputs for comparison.\n  - G2: Provide a comparison function that diffs stdout, stderr, and exit code and reports divergence with the triggering input.\n  - G3: Provide a normalization hook mechanism so individual utilities can strip or replace non-deterministic output fields before comparison.\n  - G4: Integrate with go test so that each cmd/ package can define test cases as data and call the harness from a standard TestXxx function.\n\nrequirements:\n  R1:\n    title: Test case data structure\n    items:\n      - R1.1:\n          text: |\n            Must define DiffTest as the following named struct in package testutils:\n\n              type DiffTest struct {\n                  Name          string                 // subtest name used with t.Run; required\n                  Args          []string               // command-line arguments passed to both binaries\n                  Stdin         []byte                 // nil = no stdin (both binaries receive EOF immediately)\n                  Env           []string               // nil = use defaults only (LC_ALL=C); non-nil = KEY=VALUE pairs merged into inherited environment\n                  WorkDir       string                 // empty = per-test t.TempDir(); non-empty = use this directory for both binaries\n                  ExitCode      int                    // expected exit code for both binaries\n                  Normalize     []func([]byte) []byte  // applied in order to stdout and stderr of both binaries before comparison; nil or empty = no normalization\n                  ExpectedFiles map[string][]byte      // optional: path -> expected byte content after execution, for file-output utilities (sponge, cp)\n              }\n      - R1.2:\n          text: DiffTest.Stdin nil means both binaries receive EOF immediately on stdin. An empty non-nil slice ([]byte{}) also produces no bytes but is semantically distinct (open stdin that is immediately closed).\n      - R1.3:\n          text: |\n            DiffTest.Env nil means both binaries inherit the test process environment with\n            default overrides applied (see R2.6). When non-nil, the KEY=VALUE pairs in the\n            slice are merged into the inherited environment: matching keys are overridden, new\n            keys are added. This merge behavior matches the struct comment (\"override or\n            extend\") and avoids requiring callers to replicate the full environment for a\n            single override.\n      - R1.4:\n          text: Must define type NormalizeFunc = func([]byte) []byte as a named type alias in package testutils. The Normalize field in DiffTest uses this element type. When the slice is nil or empty, no normalization is applied to stdout or stderr.\n\n  R2:\n    title: Binary execution\n    items:\n      - R2.1:\n          text: |\n            Must provide func RunDiffTests(t *testing.T, goBinary, refBinary string, tests []DiffTest) that accepts the path to the Go binary under test (goBinary) and the GNU reference binary (refBinary), then runs each DiffTest as a named subtest via t.Run(test.Name, ...). Both binaries are invoked with identical Args, Stdin, and Env for each test case.\n      - R2.2:\n          text: Must capture stdout, stderr, and exit code from each binary independently.\n      - R2.3:\n          text: Must impose a configurable timeout on each binary invocation. The default timeout must be 10 seconds. The test fails if either binary exceeds the timeout.\n      - R2.4:\n          text: Must not write any output to the test process stdout or stderr during a passing test run.\n      - R2.5:\n          text: |\n            Must support passing a working directory to each binary invocation via a WorkDir\n            field on DiffTest. When WorkDir is empty, both binaries run in a per-test temporary\n            directory created by the harness via t.TempDir(). The harness must use the same\n            working directory for both binaries in a given test case. The DiffTest struct gains:\n\n              WorkDir string // empty = per-test t.TempDir(); non-empty = use this directory for both binaries\n      - R2.6:","numLines":100,"startLine":1,"totalLines":288}}}
